# Local biomedical image review workspace

**Research proposal · 6 October 2026**

## Recommendation

Build a local-first, single-writer project workspace with a thin desktop shell around the napari viewer/layer model, a project and annotation model owned by this group, and explicitly limited readers based on `tifffile` plus Zarr. Use OME-Zarr 0.5 as the structured representation for derived label arrays and supported array exports. Keep imported originals read-only and external by default. Treat the viewer, readers and storage format as replaceable components behind a versioned project contract.

This is a bounded design recommendation, not a claim that these pins already meet the 3 GB or 16 GB targets. No application was built or benchmarked. Test the reader/viewer combination against representative lab files before fixing the package lock.

Evidence labels below distinguish **source facts**, **engineering inferences**, **product choices**, and **proposed validation**. The source catalog gives exact pins, locators and capture identities. Citations identify specific mechanisms; they do not establish full-product behavior.

## Findings that shape the design

1. **OME-NGFF supplies a useful multidimensional storage contract, with a material transform limit.** OME-NGFF 0.5.0 specifies Zarr v3 images and arrays with declared axes and per-level coordinate transformations. Its multiscale dimensions are ordered as time, channel/custom axes, then spatial axes; spatial axes may be 2D or 3D. Its defined coordinate transformations are scale followed by optional translation; the format does not encode arbitrary rotation or shear in that model. Label images use integer sample types. [S1, “Images”, “axes”, “coordinateTransformations”, “multiscales”, and “labels”](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ome/ngff-spec/0.5/index.md)

   **Inference:** store full coordinate meaning in the project contract, even when a standard exchange format cannot represent it. An export that cannot represent the stored transform must either include an explicit sidecar or perform a separately recorded resampling operation.

2. **`tifffile` documents a practical lazy-access path for TIFF, not universal TIFF support or a performance guarantee.** Its pinned README demonstrates reading OME-TIFF metadata/series, exposing image data as a Zarr array and slicing a region; it also calls out TIFF subsets, vendor extensions and codec dependencies. [S2, `README.rst`, “Usage” and format/support notes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cgohlke/tifffile/v2026.9.20/README.rst)

   **Inference:** use it for a narrowly tested OME-TIFF subset, keep source files untouched and refuse layouts whose smallest readable segment is too large for the memory budget. Lazy array access alone does not prove that every TIFF page, codec or segment is bounded.

3. **napari Shapes is a separate GUI/annotation precedent.** The v0.9.2 `Shapes` layer accepts vertex arrays in 2D or 3D, multiple geometry types and style/attribute state, and documents affine layer transforms. This contributes a reusable drawing and viewer mechanism that is independent of OME-NGFF's on-disk axis and calibration schema. [S3, `src/napari/layers/shapes/shapes.py`, `Shapes` class documentation and constructor](https://github.com/napari/napari/blob/v0.9.2/src/napari/layers/shapes/shapes.py)

   **Limit:** this API source does not prove that a project shell, our save/reopen semantics, accessibility, undo policy, responsiveness or every chosen file reader works. Pin v0.9.2 as an integration candidate, not an adopted dependency until a compatibility spike passes.

4. **A real chunked-write failure shows why storage granularity and task scheduling must agree.** Zarr-Python issue #3514 reported a Dask `to_zarr` write over a sharded array losing data: the reported 3D sum was 45,004,136 before writing and 25,746,614 on readback. The discussion identified concurrent writes to different chunks inside a shared shard as unsafe without coordination. Dask PR #12105 changed `to_zarr` to use a Zarr array's shard shape when available as the Dask write/rechunk unit (falling back to chunks), and added `test_zarr_chunk_shards_mismatch_warns`: a small 1D mismatch test that checks the rechunk shape and readback equality. The merge commit is `d5b07164359d3377fd9d2d0e7eebd134f9a3c31a`. Dask tag `2025.11.0` resolves to commit `7ffe1af8192d603fdd1f564626243f5c37075190`, whose history includes the fix. [S4 issue #3514](https://github.com/zarr-developers/zarr-python/issues/3514) · [S5 issue discussion](https://api.github.com/repos/zarr-developers/zarr-python/issues/3514/comments) · [S6 PR #12105](https://github.com/dask/dask/pull/12105) · [S7 changed code and regression test](https://api.github.com/repos/dask/dask/pulls/12105/files) · [S8 release ancestry](https://api.github.com/repos/dask/dask/compare/d5b07164359d3377fd9d2d0e7eebd134f9a3c31a...7ffe1af8192d603fdd1f564626243f5c37075190)

   **Limit and consequence:** the focused test establishes the intended aligned-shard case at the merged Dask commit; it is not a general proof of safe concurrent Zarr writes. A January 2026 issue comment reports a related 3D corruption result still present for an awkward shape (expected sum 45,004,136; readback 21,409,496). The available chain does not explain that later case enough to diagnose it. For the first version, write unsharded arrays and serialize project writes. If sharding is later needed, test aligned and irregular edge chunks with exact array equality/readback using the actual pinned stack. The issue is evidence about one Dask/Zarr write path, not proof that OME-NGFF or all Zarr writes are defective.

These precedents are independently useful: NGFF describes interoperable array metadata; `tifffile` supplies an image-file reader path; napari supplies viewer and geometry interactions; and Dask's issue/fix/test chain informs write scheduling. Their combination is a proposed architecture, not a demonstrated integrated system.

## Supported input boundary for the first useful version

**Product choice:** support local OME-TIFF and OME-Zarr 0.5 only, in read-only mode, with tested combinations of package pins and codecs. The initial safe subset is one selected image series with a 2D or 3D spatial image and optional time and channel axes, represented explicitly as axes such as `YX`, `ZYX`, `CYX`, `TCZYX`, or `TZCYX`. Accept tested integer and floating sample types. For TIFF, accept only OME metadata that maps unambiguously to that subset and a tested tile/strip organization whose largest decoded read unit is at most 64 MiB; require the decoder for its compression. For NGFF, accept one selected image group with valid v0.5 axis/scale/translation metadata and bounded, unsharded chunks. Establish whether these caps and the selected reader APIs work in the implementation spike.

These are product boundary choices, not capabilities promised by the standards or reader documentation. They intentionally exclude proprietary microscope formats, unknown/custom axes, ambiguous series selection, unknown channel/sample axes, RGB/sample axes in this first scope, unsupported codecs, invalid or contradictory calibration, NGFF transformations that the importer cannot interpret, and read units that exceed the cap. Broader formats can be added only with real fixtures and a named maintainer.

**Import review and failure behavior:** inspect headers and metadata first, then present source series, array shape/type, original axis declarations, pixel size/unit, orientation/transform, codec/layout and reader version. When an axis or calibration is ambiguous, keep the original metadata and offer explicit axis mapping and calibration correction fields with reason and timestamp. Do not silently squeeze/reorder/flip axes or guess units. If neither unambiguous interpretation nor user correction is available, mark that image import as rejected/unsupported, explain which field blocked it, and leave the rest of the project usable. Import into a staging record; only a fully validated record is promoted into the active project generation. The original image bytes are never rewritten.

## Project data contract

A versioned project directory contains a manifest, immutable source records, annotation records, derived operation records, optional label-array stores, style definitions and recoverable save generations. It is independent of any one viewer's in-memory state.

- **Identity and provenance:** each logical image has a stable project UUID plus source format, series/group selection, original path/URI, byte length, file timestamps as hints, exact source axes/shape/dtype, untouched raw source metadata, reader/package versions, import time and integrity state. Compute SHA-256 by streaming fixed-size blocks in a cancellable background task. For a directory source such as OME-Zarr, record sorted relative paths, sizes and per-file hashes and a digest of that inventory. Filename, path, shape and mtime are hints, not identity. Until the content digest is complete the UI must say “identity pending”, not “verified”. A duplicate file may appear as two logical acquisitions with distinct project IDs and one shared content hash.
- **Relocation and portability:** default to referencing the original external path so a 3 GB source is not silently copied. On another machine, missing sources remain offline until the researcher selects a replacement; verify its streaming content hash before reconnecting it. Same path with changed bytes is a mismatch. Offer an explicit portable bundle that copies the unchanged source and records its digest, trading storage and transfer time for self-containment. Copy completed, closed bundles to shared storage; edit a local working copy. A bundle with external references can reopen only if those sources are available or explicitly relocated.
- **Axes and coordinates:** preserve `array_axes` exactly as read, in storage order, e.g. `T,C,Z,Y,X`; do not treat display order as stored order. Spatial coordinates in annotations use canonical source-pixel axes `x,y` or `x,y,z`, with integer index 0 at the center of the first pixel/voxel. A per-image coordinate frame declares dimensionality, axis mapping, physical units and a float64 homogeneous matrix `index_to_world` using column vectors: `[x,y[,z],1] -> physical coordinates`. The matrix includes sampling, origin and orientation and may represent rotation/shear. Record the exact matrix and matrix convention; validate it is finite and invertible. Keep non-spatial context (`T`, `C`, and for 2D-on-volume annotations `Z`) as explicit integer selectors with the axis names. The viewer status area shows both index coordinates and physical coordinates/units and the active `T/C/Z` selectors. An isolated arithmetic witness for this proposed mapping is W1; it does not validate any image reader or viewer.
- **Annotations:** every record has UUID, geometry kind (point, path, polygon, integer mask or acquisition-level note), coordinate-frame ID, vertices or mask reference, class/label, reusable style ID, scope, named axis selectors, optional author/note and creation/edit history. A slice annotation stores 2D `x,y` vertices and explicit `Z` plus any `T/C`; a volume annotation stores 3D `x,y,z` vertices and any selected `T/C`; an acquisition-wide record has no implicit slice/time and may be non-spatial. Never infer scope from whichever plane happens to be displayed. Store mask labels as integer data with an explicit label table and frame/scope. Styles are named project/user templates, not baked into original samples.
- **Calibration corrections:** save imported raw metadata unchanged alongside a separate override with old/new values, affected axes, units, author, timestamp and explanation. The override affects project interpretation and exports, not the original source. Show a visible “corrected” marker and provide a way to inspect both values.
- **Display is a view:** channels on/off, channel colors, per-channel window/level, LUT/gamma, blend, current slice and time are project view settings. Apply intensity mapping to display buffers only, for example `clip((sample-low)/(high-low), 0, 1)`; never overwrite or cast the source array. Label original image layers, annotation layers, display composites and derived-image layers distinctly. W1 checks the stated linear mapping on three values and confirms its input list is unchanged; it is mathematical, not application evidence.
- **Geometric operations:** crop, reorientation, registration, projection, resampling or other sample-changing operations create a new child dataset and never edit the original. Record operation ID, parent/source digest, parameters, input and output axes/shapes/dtypes, output digest, interpolation and border policy, coordinate mapping and exact software pins. For an OME-NGFF export, write its scale/translation only when they faithfully express the transform. Preserve a richer transform in project metadata/sidecar or require an explicit resample to a supported grid.

## Application structure and workflow

1. **Create/open a project.** The project manager creates a versioned directory, writes a starting generation and explains whether sources will be referenced or copied. Opening runs schema migration on a new generation, never overwrites the only prior generation.
2. **Import and review.** Reader adapter probes metadata without decoding the full image. The review panel exposes raw metadata, parsed axes/calibration, codec, sampling and provenance. An unresolved image can stay as a rejected import record with its original path and error; existing images and annotations remain intact.
3. **Inspect.** Use bounded Zarr-backed reads for visible tiles/planes, a cancellable worker pool and a configurable LRU cache capped at 512 MiB. Limit each decoded read unit to 64 MiB in the supported subset. Cache entries are disposable and keyed by source digest, selected axes/indices and view request. On a 3 GB file, there must be no full-source copy or full-volume decode just to display a plane. This is a target to measure, not a consequence of using lazy arrays.
4. **Navigate and annotate.** Show dimension controls for `T`, `C` and `Z`, along with shortcut hints and keyboard focus order. Use napari Shapes for initial point/path/polygon interaction if the v0.9.2 spike verifies dimensional and affine behavior; use the project schema, not widget state, as the saved truth. Masks are integer label arrays. Record annotation edits as commands with before/after values or changed chunks so undo/redo does not require copying a large mask. Keep style presets and user-remappable keyboard bindings. UI is keyboard operable; metadata/errors have persistent labels, readable text and visible focus.
5. **Save and close.** An append-only edit journal records committed annotation/project commands. A save writes a new version into a staging directory, checksums its contents, flushes files/directories where supported, renames the complete immutable version into place, then atomically replaces a small `CURRENT` pointer on a tested local filesystem. Preserve the prior version. On startup, validate `CURRENT`, checksums and journal prefix; if incomplete, offer last-known-good version and a recoverable journal replay. Ignore/quarantine staging generations rather than treating them as committed. Save does not include disposable cache contents as data.
6. **Reopen or transfer.** Reopen the last committed project generation; verify external sources, report missing or changed hashes and let the user relocate explicitly. Schema migration writes a new generation while preserving the old. Shared storage is a transfer destination, not a concurrent multiwriter database. Detect a stale project revision and refuse silent overwrite. Do not promise atomic rename, locking or fsync semantics on every network share; qualify them on target storage or stage/copy and verify a closed bundle locally.
7. **Export and verify.** Export a long-form CSV table with project/image IDs, content digest, annotation IDs, vertex order, index coordinates, physical coordinates and units, frame ID/matrix, scope and axis selectors, labels/styles and schema version. Export masks as an integer label array plus label table and full frame/provenance sidecar; the CSV alone does not preserve mask pixels. A 2D preview overlay such as PNG is flattened display output and is not a substitute for that label data. Export a small OME-TIFF crop of original samples, preserving source dtype and selected axes where supported; its sidecar states crop bounds, updated origin, calibration, source digest and whether sample values changed. If a user chooses a windowed PNG or resampled output, state that it has changed samples and include the operation. Build each export in a temporary sibling directory and publish it only after files, hashes and sidecar agree. Show a before/after summary: original file hash unchanged; source layer untouched; what output samples/axes/calibration were retained, dropped or transformed.

For ordinary shared folders, a small group should exchange closed versioned bundles and maintain one active writer per project copy. Six people editing the same shared folder at once is outside scope; no conflict-free merge or audit-grade multi-user locking is promised. Keep the source byte hash and project revision in every export so a reviewer can detect an overlay associated with different image bytes.

## Architecture choice and bounded alternative

**Preferred choice:** embed napari's viewer and Shapes layer inside a small project shell, with separate modules for source adapters, immutable project/coordinate schema, command/undo history, storage generations, export and UI. Use `tifffile`'s documented Zarr interface for qualified OME-TIFF and the Zarr reader for qualified OME-Zarr. Use Dask only where measured/needed for chunked work; keep MVP writes serial and unsharded. Keep napari and source package pins in a lockfile after the integration spike; NGFF specification pin is 0.5.0. This reuses focused public implementations rather than owning a new multidimensional renderer, while keeping project identity and persistence under the group's control.

**Plausible alternative:** a purpose-built PySide6 desktop shell with pyqtgraph/vispy display components gives tighter control over keyboard flow, project lifecycle and supported dimensions, but leaves the group responsible for image rendering, transforms, annotation drawing and performance fixes. Choose it only if napari integration fails the needed keyboard accessibility, transform mapping or memory/latency gates. Another exchange option is OME-TIFF for small crops because it can be broadly read by existing software, but it is a less suitable authoritative store for chunked masks and project histories; keep it an export target, not the working project database.

## Atomicity and failure model

The implementation must make clear which state is committed. Imports, saves and exports are staged away from the active pointer/final export path. A crash before promotion leaves the prior project/export intact; a startup integrity pass offers recovery and retains the incomplete stage for diagnosis. Persist journal entries with sequence numbers/checksums and accept only a valid prefix. Preserve at least the last two completed generations until a later verified save. Do not rely on in-place mutation of the original file or on replacing a non-empty directory atomically. Filesystem rename/flush guarantees vary, especially on SMB/NFS; if the shared filesystem cannot pass fault-injection checks, use it only to transfer a closed, checksummed bundle and work on local storage.

Source identity is content, not filename. Stream hashes without holding source bytes in memory. Hashing can take time for 3 GB, so show progress/cancel and retain an honest pending/unverified state; require verification before reconnecting a moved source or asserting that exported annotations match it. For directory stores, verify a deterministic inventory of relative paths and bytes. If the external source is changed, leave project state and saved annotations intact but disable overlay/export association until a user imports the replacement as a new source or explicitly revises provenance.

## Exports: what survives

- **CSV:** point and vector annotation rows, index and physical coordinates, scope and selectors, IDs, source digest and frame transform. It does not carry source pixels or mask voxels.
- **Integer label store:** mask samples and label IDs on a declared array grid; include axes, scale/translation where representable, frame matrix and source digest. OME-NGFF 0.5's label requirement for integer type is a fit for this role [S1], but arbitrary affine metadata may need the project sidecar.
- **Flattened overlay:** a rendered convenience image tied to a specific selected plane/channel/time and view mapping. It loses independent layers and may lose physical calibration unless the sidecar is retained. Mark it “flattened preview”.
- **Small derived crop:** by default, byte/sample values copied unchanged for the selected spatial region; the crop changes spatial extent and origin, not the remaining sample values. The OME-TIFF and sidecar state the subset and transforms. If a codec/format cannot encode a supported dtype/calibration without conversion, fail that export clearly or offer an explicitly labeled converted preview.

## Useful opportunity and deferred ideas

**Opportunity:** stable image identity plus explicit axes/transforms and OME label output makes project data inspectable and exchangeable with other tools. A plugin-oriented viewer can also let the group add tested importers incrementally without making every vendor format an app requirement. A local manifest and sidecar retain details that a flattened picture hides.

**Optional leads, kept out of minimum obligations:** investigate OMERO for managed institutional storage and multi-user organization; evaluate registration or automatic segmentation only as provenance-recorded derived operations; revisit GPU acceleration if measured rendering bottlenecks justify it; revisit Dask/Zarr sharding only after stress tests cover concurrent writes and edge shapes; and consider project-level collaboration/version merge as a separate design. See `leads.json` for the useful leads, source status and adoption gates. None is required for the first release.

## Validation plan (all application-level checks proposed / UNEXECUTED)

1. **Import/axes corpus:** create small OME-TIFF and NGFF fixtures for each accepted axis shape, dtype, unit, codec and layout. Confirm metadata-only probe, selected coordinate mapping, exact sample readback and clear rejection/correction for unknown axis, ambiguous series, unsupported codec and oversized read unit. Verify raw metadata survives every correction. Include actual lab instruments before widening the support list.
2. **3 GB bounded access:** on an ordinary 8-core, 16 GB workstation, browse a 3 GB supported source while measuring RSS, decoded read sizes and cache use. Initial engineering gates: cache at or below 512 MiB; no decode request above 64 MiB; no source-sized second allocation; process peak below 6 GiB; cached plane/slice navigation p95 below 200 ms and a cold selected-plane first draw below 2 s; cancellation visible and responsive within 1 s at task checkpoints. These are proposed acceptance targets and need representative hardware/files. If tiled/strip reads violate the bound, exclude that layout or select another reader.
3. **Coordinate/export discrimination:** use a small 2D and 3D array with nonzero origin, anisotropic scale, axis permutation and rotation/shear. Check exact known index-to-world coordinates and inverse round trips at ≤1e-9 physical-unit error. Export CSV and label/overlay; independently parse it and prove annotation/source binding by digest. Confirm an unrepresentable affine is retained in sidecar or that export stops pending explicit resampling. W1 checks only the stated mathematical example.
4. **Display versus geometry:** adjust channel windows and confirm original bytes/hash and sample dtype remain constant; then apply one crop and one nearest-neighbor resampling to a derived child and verify exact output samples, origin and operation lineage. Test time-, slice-, volume- and acquisition-scoped annotations after close/reopen.
5. **Persistence/recovery:** fault-inject stop/kill before and after version promotion, `CURRENT` replacement, journal append, import validation and export publication on local filesystems and each chosen shared-storage type. Verify last-good generation recovery, incomplete-stage handling, hashes, no lost committed annotation edits, and that failed operations do not damage original sources. Simulate relocation, same-path changed bytes and missing/offline sources.
6. **Writer risk:** reproduce Dask #12105's aligned-shard test on the chosen dependency lock and add a 3D array with irregular final edges and multiple write tasks. Compare every sample on readback, repeat under parallel scheduling and cancellation. Until those checks pass, disable sharding and parallel writes for project data.
7. **Interaction/accessibility:** complete the full project/import/review/navigation/annotation/undo-redo/save/close/reopen/export path with keyboard only. Check focus order, shortcut discoverability, high-contrast/legible metadata and persistent textual errors. Keep actual user timing and failure observations with test fixtures/version pins.

W1 is the only candidate-executed check in this research. Its code, input, expected result, stdout/stderr/exit and limits are recorded in `witnesses.json`. It tests pure arithmetic/display mapping in an isolated Python component; it does not validate a TIFF, Zarr, viewer or application. Every other item above is proposed/UNEXECUTED.
